# Code structure: read what will judge the code, before writing it

Measured on this repository, 2026-09-18: seven identical TypeScript tasks
written against 6409 characters of structural prose produced 13 ESLint errors
across 44 files, and the same tasks written against the instruction below
produced 0 across 33, at the same token cost. Eight of the thirteen were
`code-policy/file-kind-placement` — the prose that existed to teach the file
policy is what broke it, because it never mentioned the `formatters/` and
`validators/` folders that the config requires.

**Before writing or editing code, read the checks that will judge it and write
code that already satisfies them.** The ESLint flat config, `tsconfig.json`, and
the `check`, `lint` and `test` scripts state the policy exactly; prose restates
it approximately and goes stale. Read them before the first line: a refactor to
satisfy a check you could have read costs more than the read. When a requirement
is unclear, run the check on the file rather than guessing.

Name a few of those files, but do not hand over a checklist. Measured on four
matched tasks, all three variants produced 0 errors and the cost was not
monotonic: naming three files cost 45488 tokens per task, an ordered list of
four cost 54670, and naming none at all cost 56018. Under-specifying is as
expensive as over-specifying -- the model spends the difference searching for
what to read. An earlier version of this paragraph claimed the opposite, on a
comparison where both arms named files and one task was omitted.

## Where this does not pay

For Rust, judged by `cargo baseline check` against consumer-o's `baseline.toml`, the
prose in `rust/rust-tauri.mdc` and the read-the-config instruction both produced
0 errors over 55 scored files, so replacing it would have bought nothing.
Reading was also materially slower there: one task exceeded a six-minute budget
twice under read-the-config and finished in under four minutes under the prose.

Two conditions plausibly separate that case from the TypeScript one. Whether the
prose has drifted from the checker: the TypeScript prose had, naming no
`formatters/` folder, and the Rust prose had not, because it states the same
limits `baseline.toml` sets. And how expensive the thing to read is: a crate of
1103 files is not a small package. This is a hypothesis fitted to three
languages, not a demonstrated cause -- treat it as a reason to measure each
case, never as licence to skip the measurement.

So this rule replaces prose that restates a checker approximately. Prose that
states the checker's own thresholds is earning its place: keep it, and keep it
in step with the config. Before replacing any of it, measure -- the procedure
and its traps are in the `instruction-ab-test` skill.

## The two things no config states

**Write the extension the checks actually cover.** A config that scopes typed
linting to `**/*.{ts,tsx}` does not lint `.mts`, and ESLint reports only "all of
the files matching the glob pattern are ignored" — which is not an error, so the
code ships unexamined. Match the extension the neighbouring files use.

**Where a project enforces nothing, adopt the shared toolchain rather than
inventing a local standard.** `codeality` publishes the same policy for each
language, so the fallback is to install it and then read it like any other
config: `@codeality/eslint-config` with `@codeality/tsconfig` for TypeScript,
`cargo baseline init` for Rust (it scaffolds `baseline.toml`, `clippy.toml`,
`rustfmt.toml` and `deny.toml`, and `cargo baseline check` becomes the judge),
and `codeality-py` for Python. Until one is in place the house default stands:
one file, one exported unit, one responsibility, with helpers, private
functions, inline utilities and secondary types each in their own file.
